window.HS=window.HS||{};HS.BOOKSRC=HS.BOOKSRC||{};HS.BOOKSRC["omar_techoffice_en"]={"meta":{"title":"The Architectural Technical Office","short":"The Architectural Technical Office","subtitle":"The practical guide to every project document from contract to final handover — explanation, blank form and filled example for each","by":"Eng. Omar Khamis","edition":"First edition — 2026","year":"2026","footer":"Eng. Omar Khamis — The Architectural Technical Office","editable":true,"codes":["Egyptian General Conditions","FIDIC 2017","ISO 19650","ECP","SBC"],"dedication":"To my parents — may God forgive them and reward them for all they gave me.\nAnd to every technical-office engineer who knows that a project is won or lost on paper before it is won or lost on site.","coverImg":"office.jpg","backImg":"office.jpg","blurb":"The technical office is the mind and the memory of a project: it reads the contract, turns drawings into shop drawings, gets materials approved, calls for inspections, measures quantities, prepares payment applications, and protects the rights of contractor and owner in every letter.\nThis book presents every document that passes through the technical office in the order it appears in the life of a project: from the contract summary and start-up plan, to requests for information, material submittals and inspection requests, to payment certificates, variations and claims, and on to taking-over, final handover and the final account.\nFor each document: when it is used, who prepares and reviews it, what the contract says about it by clause number, a ready blank form and a filled example from a realistic project.","about":"Eng. Omar Khamis is an Egyptian architect who has worked in design, construction, supervision and technical document management on residential and commercial projects, and the founder of the Omar Khamis Library for SketchUp, Revit, 3ds Max and AutoCAD.\nHe wrote this book for the technical-office engineer starting a first project with no one to explain why each letter is written and how each right is kept.","lang":"en"},"files":[{"n":"00_front.md","md":"#* Author's Preface\nEvery construction site is really two sites: the one you can see, with its concrete, masonry, workers and plant; and the one you cannot see, written in the contract, the drawings, the letters and the minutes. The first site builds the building. The second decides **who pays for what, when, and why**.\nThe technical office is responsible for the second site. When a project runs late, everyone asks: where is the letter in which we asked for the drawings? Where is the site handover record? Where is the sample approval? And when owner and contractor disagree on a quantity, a price or a period of time, the winner is not the one with the loudest voice but the one with **the most complete file**.\nYet most technical-office engineers start work without anyone explaining what a document is, why it is written, and how it can lose its value if it is one day late. They learn from old templates on a colleague's computer — mistakes included.\nThis book tries to close that gap. It follows **the life of a project** from the signing of the contract to final handover, and stops at each document to say: what is it? When is it used? Who prepares it and who reviews it? What does the contract say about it, **by clause number**? It then gives you a **blank form** ready to use and a **filled example** from a realistic project that shows how the boxes are completed.\nOn the contractual side I have relied on the **General Conditions for Construction Works Contracts — Model Form** issued by the Housing & Building National Research Center in Egypt, on the international **FIDIC** conditions (2017 edition), on the principles of **ISO 19650** for information management, and on the Egyptian and Saudi codes for technical matters.\n!> This book is a practical guide, not legal advice. The contract signed on your project is the binding reference, and its particular conditions may change any clause of the general conditions. In major disputes seek a lawyer specialised in construction contracts.\nI pray that this book will be of benefit, and that it will be a lasting charity for me and for my parents.\n**Eng. Omar Khamis**\n\n#* How to Use This Book\n## Order of chapters\nThe chapters follow **the order in which documents appear in the life of a project**: the start (the contract, organising the office and starting the project), then detailed design and approvals, then execution and inspections, then money (measurement, payments, variations and claims), then monitoring and reporting, and finally handover and close-out.\n## How each document is presented\nEach document in the book follows the same structure:\n1. **Definition and purpose**: what the document is and what problem it solves.\n2. **When it is used**: the event that triggers it, and any time limit.\n3. **Responsibilities**: who prepares, reviews, approves and keeps it.\n4. **Contract reference**: the corresponding clause in the Egyptian general conditions and in FIDIC.\n5. **Blank form**: ready to print or copy.\n6. **Filled example**: from a realistic project, with figures and dates.\n7. **Common mistakes**: what makes the document lose its value.\n## Boxes and symbols\nCODE> **Contract reference**: the text or summary of a clause with its number, for example: Egyptian General Conditions — Article (17).\nKEY> **Key point**: the most important thing to remember.\n!> **Warning**: a mistake that costs money or a right.\nTIP> **Site tip**: practical experience.\nEX> **Example**: a worked case.\n## The example project\nAll filled examples in the book come from one hypothetical project, so you can follow the documents as they accumulate:\nT: Data of the example project used in the forms of this book\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Project | Residential and office building — 6 floors + basement — New Cairo |\n| Project code | NCR-26 |\n| Owner (Employer) | Elite Real Estate Development Co. |\n| Consultant (the Engineer) | Integrated Engineering Office — Resident Engineer: Eng. Hossam Nabil |\n| Contractor | Modern Building Contracting Co. |\n| Project Manager (Contractor) | Eng. Khaled Saeed |\n| Technical Office Manager (Contractor) | Eng. Omar Khamis |\n| Contract value | EGP 48,600,000 (finishing and MEP works) |\n| Time for completion | 14 months |\n| Commencement date | 01/03/2026 |\n| Contract type | Unit rates with approximate quantities (re-measurement) — Egyptian General Conditions + Particular Conditions |\nREF: Housing & Building National Research Center — Egyptian General Specifications for Works Items: General Conditions for Construction Works Contracts (Model Form), 5th edition, Ministry of Housing and Utilities, Cairo.\nREF: FIDIC — Conditions of Contract for Construction (Red Book), 2nd edition, 2017.\nREF: ISO 19650-1 and -2:2018, Organization and digitization of information about buildings and civil engineering works, including BIM.\n"},{"n":"01_role.md","md":"# The Technical Office: Role, Organisation and Tools\n@image: img/omar_techoffice/ch01.jpg | The technical office: where the contract and the drawings become a daily plan of work\n@lead: The technical office is not \"the drawing department\" or \"the paperwork department\". It is the function that turns the contract into a plan, the drawings into buildable instructions, completed work into money due, and every event on site into a record that protects whoever is entitled. This chapter explains what the technical office does, how it is organised, and the tools and registers it cannot work without.\n@goals: The difference between the technical office of the contractor, the consultant and the owner | The core functions of the technical office and its links with site, quality and procurement | Organisation chart and a short job description for each member | Document numbering and review codes based on ISO 19650 | The essential registers of the technical office | The transmittal, with a blank form and a filled example\n@refs: Egyptian General Conditions for Construction Works Contracts — Article (1) Definitions and Article (17) \"Instructions to be in writing\" | FIDIC Red Book 2017 — Sub-Clause 1.3 Notices and Other Communications | ISO 19650-2:2018 — Delivery phase of the assets, information containers and naming\n\n## What is the technical office?\nEvery construction project has three main parties: **the owner** (Employer) who pays, **the consultant** (the Engineer) who designs, supervises and approves, and **the contractor** who builds. On medium and large projects each party has a technical office:\nT: The technical office of each party to the project\n| Party | The technical office handles | Main documents it issues |\n|---|---|---|\n| Contractor | Planning, shop drawings, submittals, inspection requests, measurement and payment applications, claims | Shop drawings, submittals, IRs, RFIs, payment applications, programme, letters |\n| Consultant (the Engineer) | Reviewing and approving what the contractor submits, issuing instructions and changes, certifying payments | Review responses, written instructions, variation orders, payment certificates, taking-over certificates |\n| Owner | Budget, contracts and payments; approving changes that affect cost | Approval letters, direct purchase orders, nominated subcontracts |\nIn this book we mostly write from **the point of view of the contractor's technical office**, because it handles the most documents, while explaining what the consultant does with each document.\nKEY> Every day of the project, the technical office works on **three questions**: What does the contract say? What actually happened on site? And is the difference between them recorded in writing?\nGL: المكتب الفني | Technical office | The function responsible for the technical and contractual documents, planning and measurement of a project\nGL: رب العمل | Employer / Owner | The party that contracts for the works and pays for them\nGL: المهندس | The Engineer | The person or firm appointed by the Employer to administer the contract and supervise the works\n\n## Functions of the technical office\n### 1. Understanding and administering the contract\nReading the contract and all its documents and extracting the **obligations and time limits** into one summary (Chapter 2), then monitoring compliance throughout the project: programme submission dates, review periods, claim notice periods, payment dates.\n### 2. Planning and monitoring\nPreparing the baseline **programme**, look-ahead plans, tracking actual progress, writing periodic reports and giving early warning of delay.\n### 3. Construction and shop drawings\nTurning contract drawings into **shop drawings** that can be built from, coordinating the disciplines (architectural, structural and MEP), and preparing **as-built drawings** at the end.\n### 4. Submittals\nSubmitting **materials and samples**, manufacturers' data, suppliers and subcontractors for approval before delivery or execution.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (19), paragraph 2**: Before delivering materials to site the contractor shall submit to the Engineer, at his own cost, **samples of the materials** he intends to supply for approval, with a written statement of the source and producer and any specification or information the Engineer requests. Approved samples are sealed and kept in a safe place for checking deliveries against them; approval of samples does not relieve the contractor of his responsibility.\n### 5. Inspections and quality\nIssuing **inspection requests** for work before it is covered and for delivered materials, and following up and closing **non-conformance reports**, together with the quality engineer.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (20) \"Examination of work before covering up\"**: The contractor shall not cover up or conceal any work before the Engineer's approval, and shall notify the Engineer **in writing** whenever work is ready to be covered or concealed. If the Engineer does not carry out the examination at the agreed time, the contractor may send the Engineer the result of the examination he has carried out himself.\n### 6. Measurement and payments\n**Measuring quantities** executed, preparing **monthly payment applications**, following up payment, and preparing the **final account**.\n### 7. Variations and claims\nRecording any instruction or circumstance that changes the scope, cost or time of the work, submitting **variation requests** and **claims** within the contractual time limits, and pricing additional work.\n### 8. Correspondence and document control\nReceiving and issuing every letter, drawing and document, and **numbering, registering and archiving** them so that any document can be found within minutes, even years later.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (17) \"Instructions to be in writing\"**: All instructions or approvals of the Engineer or his representative shall be **in writing**, with a copy to the Employer. If oral instructions are given, the contractor shall carry them out, and they shall be confirmed in writing as soon as possible by the Engineer or his representative, or the contractor shall confirm them in writing to the Engineer with a copy to the Employer; **if neither the Employer nor the Engineer objects within seven days** of receiving the contractor's letter, the contractor's confirmation is deemed a written instruction of the Engineer.\nEX> During a site walk the resident engineer said: \"Raise the bathroom floors by 5 cm.\" This is an oral instruction affecting waterproofing, masonry and doors. The same day the technical office writes a \"confirmation of oral instruction\" letter stating the words of the instruction, its date and who gave it, and sends it to the consultant with a copy to the owner. After 7 days with no objection it is a written instruction — and the contractor is entitled to claim its cost if it is a variation.\n\n## Organisation\nThe size of the technical office depends on the project: on a villa it may be one engineer who does everything; on a tower or a hospital it may be twenty people. But the **functions** are always the same.\n```fig 660x400\n[[\"r\",250,10,160,46,\"Project Manager\",\"dark\"],[\"r\",250,90,160,46,\"Technical Office Manager\",\"zone\"],[\"l\",330,56,330,90],[\"l\",70,160,600,160],[\"l\",330,136,330,160],[\"r\",10,170,120,56,\"Planning engineer\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",140,170,120,56,\"QS / cost engineer\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",270,170,120,56,\"Shop drawing & coordination engineer\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",400,170,120,56,\"Submittals engineer\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",530,170,120,56,\"Document controller\",\"glass\"],[\"l\",70,160,70,170],[\"l\",200,160,200,170],[\"l\",330,160,330,170],[\"l\",460,160,460,170],[\"l\",590,160,590,170],[\"r\",270,250,120,46,\"Draughtsmen: arch. / struct. / MEP\",\"stone\"],[\"l\",330,226,330,250],[\"r\",10,330,190,50,\"Site engineers (execution)\",\"green\"],[\"r\",235,330,190,50,\"QA/QC engineer\",\"green\"],[\"r\",460,330,190,50,\"Procurement and stores\",\"green\"],[\"l\",105,330,105,316,\"dash\",\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"l\",105,316,555,316,\"dash\",\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"l\",555,316,555,330,\"dash\",\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"l\",330,296,330,330,\"dash\",\"#1d5fa8\"],[\"t\",330,395,\"Dashed line: daily coordination and exchange of documents\",11,\"#1d5fa8\"]]\n```\n: Typical organisation of a contractor's technical office and its links with the other project functions\nT: Short job descriptions of technical office members\n| Position | Main responsibilities | Key outputs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Technical Office Manager | Reading the contract, supervising all outputs, contractual correspondence, claims | Contract summary, formal letters, claims file |\n| Planning engineer | Programme and updates, look-aheads, progress reports, delay analysis | Baseline and updated programmes, monthly reports |\n| QS / cost engineer | Measurement, payment applications, subcontractor accounts, pricing of extras | Monthly application, measurement books, rate build-ups |\n| Shop drawing & coordination engineer | Shop drawings and coordination between disciplines, as-built drawings | Shop drawings, coordination drawings |\n| Submittals engineer | Preparing, submitting and following up material and subcontractor approvals | Submittal packages, submittal log |\n| Document controller | Receiving, sending, registering and archiving all documents; distributing new revisions and withdrawing old ones | Correspondence register, drawing register, transmittals |\nTIP> On small projects one engineer does all of these. Keep a weekly timetable that gives time to each function, otherwise shop drawings and measurement will swallow your time and letters will pile up unanswered.\n\n## How documents flow between the parties\nThe golden rule: **every document leaving the technical office leaves with a numbered transmittal, and every document arriving is registered with its date of receipt**. A technical document usually follows this path:\n```fig 660x300\n[[\"r\",10,110,140,70,\"Contractor: prepares document\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",200,110,140,70,\"Transmittal + registration\",\"stone\"],[\"r\",390,110,140,70,\"Consultant: review\",\"zone\"],[\"r\",560,40,95,50,\"A Approved\",\"green\"],[\"r\",560,100,95,50,\"B Approved as noted\",\"green\"],[\"r\",560,160,95,50,\"C Revise & resubmit\",\"red\"],[\"r\",560,220,95,50,\"D Rejected\",\"red\"],[\"ar\",150,145,200,145],[\"ar\",340,145,390,145],[\"ar\",530,130,560,65],[\"ar\",530,140,560,125],[\"ar\",530,150,560,185],[\"ar\",530,160,560,245],[\"pl\",607,210,607,290,80,290,80,180,{\"s\":\"#b3402f\",\"d\":\"6,4\",\"w\":1.5}],[\"t\",340,285,\"C and D: the document is revised and resubmitted as a new revision (Rev.1, Rev.2…)\",11,\"#b3402f\"]]\n```\n: Path of a technical document from contractor to consultant and the four review codes\n### Review status codes\nT: Common review codes for documents submitted for approval\n| Code | Meaning | What the contractor does |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | Approved | Proceeds with procurement or execution |\n| B | Approved as noted | Proceeds in compliance with the comments, and resubmits if asked |\n| C | Revise and resubmit | **Does not proceed**; revises and resubmits as a new revision |\n| D | Rejected | Does not proceed; submits a different alternative |\n!> \"Approved as noted\" is not \"approved\". Most problems happen when site builds from a code-B drawing and ignores the comments written on it. Issue the drawing to site **with** the comment sheet, or reissue it with the comments incorporated.\n\n## Document numbering\nA document without a number is a lost document. **ISO 19650** recommends that every \"information container\" (file, drawing or letter) has a unique name made of fixed fields separated by hyphens. It can be simplified for our projects as follows:\nT: A proposed document numbering system (based on the principles of ISO 19650-2)\n| Field | Meaning | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Project | Short fixed code | NCR26 |\n| Originator | Who issued the document | CON (contractor), ENG (consultant), EMP (owner) |\n| Discipline | Technical field | AR architectural, ST structural, ME mechanical, EL electrical, PL plumbing, GN general |\n| Document type | What it is | LT letter, SD shop drawing, MS material submittal, IR inspection request, RFI request for information, MOM minutes, VO variation, IPC payment application |\n| Number | Sequential for each type | 0001, 0002 … |\n| Revision | Revision number | R0, R1, R2 … |\nEX> `NCR26-CON-AR-SD-0042-R1` = project NCR26, issued by the contractor, architectural, shop drawing, number 42, second issue (after a code-C response to the first). From the name alone anyone knows what the file is without opening it.\nTIP> Do not put dates or words such as \"final\" and \"final-final\" in file names. The date belongs inside the document and in the register; the \"final\" is the latest approved revision in the drawing register.\nGL: خطاب الإرسال | Transmittal | A numbered document accompanying any documents sent, proving what was sent, to whom, when and for what purpose\nGL: الإصدار | Revision | A number identifying each amended issue of a document\nGL: كود الرد | Review status code | The classification of the consultant's response to a submitted document (A/B/C/D)\n\n## Registers of the technical office\nA register (log) is a table updated daily that answers one question: **where has each document got to?** These are the registers no technical office can work without:\nT: The essential registers of the technical office\n| Register | What it tracks | Key columns |\n|---|---|---|\n| Incoming and outgoing correspondence | Every letter in or out | No., date, from/to, subject, reply required?, reply date |\n| Drawing register | Every contract and shop drawing with its revisions | Drawing no., title, current revision, date, code, distribution |\n| Submittal log | Every sample, material and subcontractor submitted | No., item, date submitted, date answered, code, review days |\n| RFI log | Every question to the consultant | No., question, date sent, required by, date answered, impact |\n| Inspection request (IR) log | Every inspection of work and materials | No., item, location, date, result, resubmission no. |\n| Non-conformance (NCR) log | Every formally recorded defect | No., description, corrective action, date closed |\n| Variation (VO) log | Every change instruction or additional work | No., description, estimated value, approved value, time impact |\n| Notices and claims register | Every notice of an event that may lead to a claim | Event, date known, date notified, time limit, status |\nKEY> A good register shows **late responses** automatically: the column \"review days\" = date answered − date submitted. When the consultant exceeds the contractual review period, the register is the first evidence in any claim for extension of time.\n\n## A day in the life of a technical office engineer\n1. **Morning**: review yesterday's incoming documents, register and distribute them, and go through today's inspection requests with the site engineers.\n2. **A short site walk**: what was built yesterday? Is any work about to be covered without inspection? Has anything been built differently from the drawings?\n3. **Midday**: shop drawings, measurement and preparing submittals.\n4. **End of day**: answer letters, update the registers, write the daily report and send tomorrow's inspection requests.\nTIP> Make \"Was any oral instruction given today that is not yet in writing?\" a fixed question you ask the site engineers at the end of every day.\n\n:::form Document Transmittal\n| Transmittal no. | ____ | Date | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| From | ____ | To | ____ |\n| Project | ____ | Attention | ____ |\nT:\n| No. | Document no. | Title | Revision | Copies | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | ____ | ____ | ____ | ____ | ____ |\n| 2 | ____ | ____ | ____ | ____ | ____ |\n| 3 | ____ | ____ | ____ | ____ | ____ |\nPurpose: [ ] For approval   [ ] For information   [ ] For construction   [ ] For comment   [ ] As requested\nRemarks: ____\n| Sent by (name and signature) | ____ | Received by (name, signature and date) | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\n\n:::filled Document Transmittal\n| Transmittal no. | NCR26-CON-GN-TR-0118 | Date | 12/04/2026 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| From | Modern Building — Technical Office | To | Integrated Engineering Office (Consultant) |\n| Project | NCR-26 Residential and office building — New Cairo | Attention | Eng. Hossam Nabil — Resident Engineer |\n| No. | Document no. | Title | Revision | Copies | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | NCR26-CON-AR-SD-0042 | Shop drawing — typical floor bathrooms — plans and sections | R1 | 2 paper + PDF | For approval |\n| 2 | NCR26-CON-AR-SD-0043 | Shop drawing — bathroom waterproofing details | R0 | 2 paper + PDF | For approval |\n| 3 | NCR26-CON-AR-MS-0017 | Material submittal — 60×60 porcelain floor tiles (sample + catalogue + certificates) | R0 | 1 + 2 samples | For approval |\nPurpose: [x] For approval   [ ] For information   [ ] For construction   [ ] For comment   [ ] As requested\nRemarks: Revision R1 of drawing 0042 incorporates the comments of your response ENG-LT-0057 dated 05/04/2026 (code C). Please respond within the contractual period (14 days), as the drawings are linked to the waterproofing activity scheduled for 03/05/2026.\n| Sent by (name and signature) | Eng. Omar Khamis | Received by (name, signature and date) | Ms Mona Samir — 12/04/2026 — 11:20 am |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\nEX> Note three things in the example: (1) the link to the previous response by number and date, (2) the contractual review period, (3) the link to a programme activity and its date. These three sentences make any late response claimable later.\n\n## Common mistakes\nT: Organisational mistakes that cost the project\n| Mistake | Result | Prevention |\n|---|---|---|\n| Carrying out oral instructions without written confirmation | Loss of the right to claim cost and time | A confirmation letter the same day (Article 17: 7 days) |\n| Old drawings on site | Wrong work and breaking out | Withdraw superseded revisions by letter and stamp \"superseded\" |\n| Documents without numbers or transmittals | No proof of submission or its date | Numbered transmittal and signed receipt |\n| Registers not updated | Nobody knows what is outstanding | Daily updates and a weekly review in the technical office meeting |\n| Treating code B as a full approval | Work contrary to the comments | Issue the comments with the drawing or reissue it |\n\nGL: سجل اللوحات | Drawing register | A table of all project drawings, their current revisions and approval status\nGL: طلب معلومات | Request for Information (RFI) | A written question to the consultant to clarify a conflict or gap in the documents\nGL: تقرير عدم مطابقة | Non-Conformance Report (NCR) | A document recording work or material that does not comply with the specification and requiring corrective action\n"},{"n":"02_contract.md","md":"# Contract Documents: Reading and Summarising the Contract\n@image: img/omar_techoffice/ch02.jpg | A contract is not a file to keep in a drawer; it is the map of the project\n@lead: The first task of the technical office after signing is to read the whole contract — every document in it — and turn it into one sheet from which everyone knows what we owe and when, and what we are owed and when. This chapter goes through the contract documents one by one, their order of precedence when they conflict, and how to extract a contract summary and a table of contractual time limits.\n@goals: The core contract definitions in the Egyptian general conditions | The contract documents and their order of precedence in the Egyptian conditions and in FIDIC 2017 | What to extract from each document: conditions, drawings, specification, bill of quantities | Types of contract by pricing method | The contract summary and the table of time limits, with a blank form and a filled example | Dealing with conflicts between documents\n@refs: Egyptian General Conditions for Construction Works Contracts — Articles (1) Definitions, (2) Language, (3) Governing law, (4) Contract documents and their order, (23) Drawings, (32) Performance security, (46) Programme, (48) Commencement date, (53) Delay damages, (75) Measurement, (77) Monthly statements, (86) Defects period, (87) Final handover | FIDIC Red Book 2017 — Sub-Clauses 1.1 Definitions, 1.5 Priority of Documents, 1.9 Delayed Drawings or Instructions\n\n## Core definitions\nThe first pages of any contract are the **definitions**, and they are not a formality: the word \"Engineer\", \"Site\" or \"Works\" in any later clause means exactly what it is defined to mean here.\nT: Key definitions of the Egyptian General Conditions — Article (1)\n| Term | Short definition |\n|---|---|\n| Employer | The first party: the owner named in the contract agreement or his legal successor |\n| Contractor | The second party whose tender the Employer accepted or with whom he contracted |\n| Subcontractor | Anyone to whom the contractor entrusts part of the works, subject to the Employer's consent |\n| Engineer | The natural or legal person appointed by the Employer to act as Engineer under the contract |\n| Engineer's representative | The person appointed by the Engineer to carry out the duties in Article (15) |\n| Contract | The contract agreement, general and particular conditions, specification, drawings, priced bills of quantities, tender, letter of acceptance and any other document stated to form part of the contract |\n| Bills of quantities | Lists describing the work items with **approximate** quantities and their rates |\n| Permanent works | All works to be executed and handed over provisionally and finally under the contract |\n| Temporary works | The plant, equipment, tools and gear needed to execute the permanent works |\n| Completion of the works | Finishing and testing the works so that they are fit for their intended purpose and can be taken over provisionally |\n| Time for completion | The original period in the contract agreement plus extensions approved by the Engineer, counted from the commencement date |\n| Site | The places or land — **free of obstructions** — designated in the contract for the works, including areas for offices, stores and storage |\n| Retention | Amounts withheld by the Employer from the contractor's payments and released on the stated dates |\n| Written notice | Any written document issued by either party or its representative and received by the other against signature |\nKEY> Note that the bills of quantities in the Egyptian conditions are **approximate**: the contractor is paid for the quantities **actually executed** at the contract rates (Article 7: the contract value is the value obtained by applying the rates to the quantities actually executed). **Accurate measurement** is therefore one of the most important tasks of the technical office.\nGL: الإخطار الكتابي | Written notice | A written document from one party to the contract received by the other against signature\nGL: الأعمال المؤقتة | Temporary works | What is needed to build and does not remain in the building after handover\n\n## Contract documents and their order of precedence\nA contract is not a single sheet but a set of documents. When two documents conflict — say the specification says \"porcelain 60×60\" and the drawing says \"60×120\" — the contract itself decides **which prevails**.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (4) \"Contract documents, their order and interpretation\"**: All the documents forming the contract are one integrated whole, each explaining and completing the others. They take precedence in interpretation in the following order: (1) the contract agreement, (2) the letter of acceptance/award, (3) the tender, (4) Part Two of the conditions (particular conditions), (5) Part One of the conditions (general conditions), (6) the construction drawings, (7) the general technical specification, (8) the priced bills of quantities, (9) any documents or correspondence exchanged before signing that the parties agree to include in the contract.\nT: Order of precedence of contract documents — Egyptian conditions vs FIDIC 2017\n| Priority | Egyptian General Conditions — Art. (4) | FIDIC Red Book 2017 — Sub-Clause 1.5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Contract agreement | The Contract Agreement |\n| 2 | Letter of acceptance / award | The Letter of Acceptance |\n| 3 | Tender | The Letter of Tender |\n| 4 | Particular conditions | Particular Conditions — Part A |\n| 5 | General conditions | Particular Conditions — Part B |\n| 6 | Construction drawings | The General Conditions |\n| 7 | General technical specification | The Specification |\n| 8 | Priced bills of quantities | The Drawings |\n| 9 | Documents and correspondence agreed before signing | The Schedules, JV Undertaking and any other documents |\n!> Note the important difference: in the Egyptian conditions **drawings precede the specification**, while in FIDIC **the specification precedes the drawings**. Never assume the order; read the clause in your contract, because the particular conditions may change it.\nTIP> The order of precedence is used **only to resolve a genuine conflict**. Before deciding which document prevails, send a **request for information (RFI)** to the consultant explaining the conflict and proposing a solution; the intention may be clear from the context of the project, and a written answer settles the matter and protects both parties.\n```fig 640x330\n[[\"r\",20,20,600,34,\"1 Contract agreement\",\"dark\"],[\"r\",50,60,540,32,\"2 Letter of acceptance / award\",\"stone\"],[\"r\",80,98,480,32,\"3 Tender\",\"stone\"],[\"r\",110,136,420,32,\"4 Particular conditions\",\"zone\"],[\"r\",140,174,360,32,\"5 General conditions\",\"zone\"],[\"r\",170,212,300,32,\"6 Construction drawings\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",200,250,240,32,\"7 Technical specification\",\"glass\"],[\"r\",230,288,180,32,\"8 Priced bills of quantities\",\"green\"],[\"t\",620,320,\"Higher prevails in a conflict\",11,\"#6b6152\",0,\"end\"]]\n```\n: The pyramid of precedence of contract documents under Article (4) of the Egyptian General Conditions\n\n## What to extract from each document\n### Contract agreement and letter of acceptance\n- **The parties**, their representatives and the addresses for formal notices.\n- **Contract value** and type (unit rates or lump sum).\n- **Time for completion** and how the commencement date is determined.\n- Any **qualifications or amendments** to the tender agreed in the letter of acceptance.\n### Particular conditions\nThis is where the details that **change** the general conditions are found. Look specifically for:\n- The rate of **delay damages** and their cap (Article 53 refers to the particular conditions).\n- The rate of **retention** deducted from each payment and its cap (Article 77).\n- The **advance payment** and its recovery rate (Article 76).\n- The period for submitting the **programme** (Article 46).\n- The amount of the **performance security** and the deadline for it (Article 32).\n- The **defects period** (Article 86) and required insurances (Article 73).\n- Any **price adjustment** rules.\n- The **dispute resolution** method (Article 92).\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (32) \"Performance security\"**: Within **twenty-one days** of the acceptance of his tender, the contractor shall provide the performance security stated in the letter of acceptance/award to guarantee performance and compliance with the contract; the bank guarantee remains valid throughout execution and until the date of final handover of the works.\n### Drawings\n- The **list of drawings** issued and the revision of each (copied into the drawing register).\n- **Conflicts** between disciplines (architectural/structural/MEP) — these become RFIs.\n- **Gaps**: details needed for construction that are missing.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (23) \"Drawings\"**: The Employer shall provide the contractor with two sets of drawings free of charge, and the contractor shall keep a complete set on site. If the contractor finds that additional data or drawings are needed, he shall **request them in writing in good time**, stating what is required, its priority and the date needed according to the programme; if the Engineer does not provide them in time and this is shown to have delayed or disrupted the works or caused the contractor additional cost, **the contractor is entitled to an extension of time and to compensation**. After completion the contractor shall hand the Engineer all drawings marked up with every change made during construction.\n### Technical specification\n- Materials **specified by name** or \"or equal\" (and the conditions for accepting an alternative).\n- **Tests required** and who pays for them (Article 19: the contractor bears the cost of tests provided for in the contract).\n- **Samples and mock-ups** required before execution.\n- **Warranties** required from manufacturers (waterproofing, aluminium, equipment).\n### Bill of quantities\n- The **method of measurement** for each item (square metre, linear metre, number, lump sum).\n- **What the rate includes**: does the tile rate include the skirting? Does the paint rate include the filler? Read the **item description** word by word.\n- Items with **doubtful quantities** (much larger or smaller than the drawings show) — they affect cash flow and the right to a new rate.\n- **Provisional sums** and nominated subcontract items.\nEX> A BOQ item reads: \"m² supply and install first-grade 60×60 porcelain tiles to specification and drawings, the rate to include bedding, grouting and skirting\". The skirting is **included** in the square-metre rate of the floor; it is not measured separately and cannot be claimed as an extra. On a medium project this short phrase is worth hundreds of thousands.\n\n## Types of contract by pricing method\nT: The main types of construction contract by pricing method\n| Type | How the contractor is paid | Main risk carried by | Key task of the technical office |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Unit rates with approximate quantities (re-measurement) | Rate × quantity actually executed | Owner (quantities) | Accurate, continuous measurement |\n| Lump sum | Fixed total sum for a defined scope | Contractor (quantities) | Controlling scope and catching every change to it |\n| Cost plus | Actual cost + fee | Owner | Documenting every cost |\n| Design and build | Usually a lump sum; the contractor is responsible for design | Contractor (design) | Design approvals and compliance with the Employer's requirements |\n| Management contracting | Management fee | Owner | Performance and cost reports |\n\n## The table of contractual time limits\nThe most dangerous parts of a contract are its **time limits**: a period that, once missed, loses a right or triggers a penalty. The technical office extracts them into one table, posted in the office and reviewed weekly.\nT: Main time limits in the Egyptian General Conditions (always check the particular conditions)\n| Event | Time limit | Article |\n|---|---|---|\n| Providing the performance security | 21 days from acceptance of the tender | 32 |\n| Submitting the detailed programme | Period in the particular conditions after notice to commence | 46 |\n| Commencement date | Date of handing over the site free of obstructions or of paying the advance, **whichever is later**, provided the building permit has been issued | 48 |\n| Objection to an oral instruction confirmed in writing by the contractor | 7 days, otherwise it is deemed a written instruction | 17 |\n| Notice to the contractor of an inspection or test | At least 3 days before the date | 19 |\n| Contractor attending to check the measurement books | 14 days from notice; written objection within 14 days | 75 |\n| Engineer's review of the monthly statement | 20 days from receipt | 77 |\n| Payment after the Engineer's notification | 10 days | 77 |\n| Submitting the final statement | 3 months from the taking-over certificate | 78 |\n| Review of the final statement | 60 days from receipt | 78 |\n| Final handover | One year after provisional handover (or as in the particular conditions) | 87 |\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (48) \"Commencement date\"**: The date of handing over the site to the contractor free of obstructions and occupation, or the date of paying the advance payment to the contractor, **whichever is later**, is the commencement date of the works, provided the Employer has obtained the building permit.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (53) \"Delay damages\"**: If the contractor fails to complete and hand over the whole of the works within the contract period, the Employer may impose delay damages at the rates or amounts stated in the particular conditions, as soon as the delay occurs and without any notice or warning. Periods of stoppage or delay that the Engineer finds were caused by matters beyond the contractor's control, and extensions approved by the Engineer, are excluded. If part of the works is taken over, damages are calculated only on the value of the unfinished works, provided these do not prevent use of the project.\nKEY> Note Article (53): damages apply **automatically**, without notice. The only way to protect the contractor against damages for a delay he did not cause is to **request an extension of time in writing at the time** (Article 50) — the subject of the chapter on claims.\n\n## The contract summary\nThe contract summary is one or two sheets that the project manager and site engineers read to know everything they need without opening the contract file. The technical office prepares it in the **first week** after signing.\n\n:::form Contract Summary\n| Project | ____ | Contract no. | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Employer / representative | ____ | Engineer (consultant) / representative | ____ |\n| Contract type | ____ | Contract value | ____ |\n| Date of signing | ____ | Commencement date and basis | ____ |\n| Time for completion | ____ | Contractual completion date | ____ |\nT:\n| Item | Value / condition | Article / reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| Performance security (amount / validity) | ____ | ____ |\n| Advance payment (rate / recovery) | ____ | ____ |\n| Retention (rate / cap) | ____ | ____ |\n| Delay damages (rate / cap) | ____ | ____ |\n| Early completion bonus | ____ | ____ |\n| Review of statement / payment period | ____ | ____ |\n| Review period for submittals and drawings | ____ | ____ |\n| Programme submission deadline | ____ | ____ |\n| Defects period | ____ | ____ |\n| Required insurances | ____ | ____ |\n| Price adjustment | ____ | ____ |\n| Dispute resolution | ____ | ____ |\n| Addresses for formal notices | ____ | ____ |\nMain risks and remarks: ____\n| Prepared by (technical office) | ____ | Approved by (project manager) | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\n\n:::filled Contract Summary\n| Project | NCR-26 Residential and office building — New Cairo | Contract no. | NE-2026-031 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Employer / representative | Elite Real Estate Development Co. / Eng. Tarek Fouad | Engineer (consultant) / representative | Integrated Engineering Office / Eng. Hossam Nabil (resident engineer) |\n| Contract type | Unit rates — approximate quantities (re-measurement) | Contract value | EGP 48,600,000 including taxes |\n| Date of signing | 10/02/2026 | Commencement date and basis | 01/03/2026 — payment of the advance (later than site handover on 22/02/2026) — Art. 48 |\n| Time for completion | 14 months | Contractual completion date | 30/04/2027 |\n| Item | Value / condition | Article / reference |\n|---|---|---|\n| Performance security (amount / validity) | 5% = EGP 2,430,000 — until final handover | Art. 32 + PC 4 |\n| Advance payment (rate / recovery) | 10% against a bank guarantee — recovered at 10% of each statement | Art. 76 + PC 7 |\n| Retention (rate / cap) | 5% of each statement up to 5% of the contract value | Art. 77 + PC 8 |\n| Delay damages (rate / cap) | 0.5% per week of the value of late works — cap 10% | Art. 53 + PC 11 |\n| Early completion bonus | None | Art. 54 + PC 12 |\n| Review of statement / payment period | 20 days / 10 days | Art. 77 |\n| Review period for submittals and drawings | 14 days (PC) | PC 15 |\n| Programme submission deadline | 21 days from notice to commence | Art. 46 + PC 9 |\n| Defects period | 12 months from provisional handover — 10 years for waterproofing (manufacturer's warranty) | Art. 86 + specification |\n| Required insurances | Contractor's all risks + third-party liability + employer's liability | Art. 73 |\n| Price adjustment | None (fixed prices) | PC 14 |\n| Dispute resolution | Amicable, then arbitration — Cairo Regional Centre for International Commercial Arbitration | Art. 92 + PC 18 |\n| Addresses for formal notices | Employer: 90th Street, New Cairo — Consultant: 14 Mosaddak St, Dokki | Art. 1 |\nMain risks and remarks: (1) The BOQ has no item for roof thermal insulation although it is shown on the drawings — RFI to be sent. (2) The specification names one brand of bathroom mixers \"or equal\" — prepare two alternatives for early approval because of long delivery. (3) 14-day review period: any late response is logged in the submittal log and notified.\n| Prepared by (technical office) | Eng. Omar Khamis — 16/02/2026 | Approved by (project manager) | Eng. Khaled Saeed — 17/02/2026 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\nTIP> Post the time-limit table beside your desk, and enter every time limit as a reminder in the technical office's shared calendar three days before it falls due.\n\n## Dealing with conflicts and gaps in the documents\n1. **Record** the conflict as soon as you find it: where? Which two documents conflict? What is the effect on execution and cost?\n2. **Do not choose** the cheapest or easiest solution on your own. Send an **RFI** that explains the conflict, proposes a solution and states a **required response date** linked to the programme.\n3. **Follow up the answer**: if it is late and affects the programme, it is a basis for an extension of time (Article 23, paragraph 5).\n4. **If the answer changes the cost** (up or down), record it in the variation log and start the variation procedure.\n!> The most dangerous phrase in a technical office: \"We'll build it and see.\" Implementing a solution to a conflict without a written answer leaves the contractor carrying the consequences if the consultant later rejects it.\n\nGL: الشروط الخاصة | Particular conditions | The part of the contract that amends or supplements the general conditions for a specific project\nGL: قوائم الكميات | Bill of Quantities (BOQ) | Lists of work items with descriptions, approximate quantities and rates\nGL: التأمين النهائي | Performance security | A bank guarantee provided by the contractor to secure proper performance\nGL: تأمين الأعمال | Retention money | A percentage withheld from each payment and released after handover\nGL: غرامة التأخير | Delay damages | An amount deducted from the contractor for each period of delay beyond the completion date\nGL: المبالغ الاحتياطية | Provisional sums | Sums in the BOQ spent on the Engineer's instructions for work not defined at contract stage\n"},{"n":"03_startup.md","md":"# Starting the Project: From Signing to the First Day of Work\n@image: img/omar_techoffice/ch03.jpg | The first six weeks set the rhythm of the whole project\n@lead: Between signing the contract and the first pour or the first course of masonry there are only a few weeks, but they are the most important in the life of a project. In them the site is handed over and the commencement date fixed, the programme submitted and approved, the staff appointed, long-lead submittals made and drawing conflicts discovered. An office that organises these weeks well spends the rest of the project monitoring; one that neglects them spends it fire-fighting.\n@goals: Contractual obligations at the start: site handover, commencement date, programme, cash flow, staff, setting out | The list of mobilisation documents for the first 30 days | Studying the documents and finding conflicts early | The request for information (RFI): how to write and follow it up — blank form and filled example | The kick-off meeting: agenda and filled minutes | The plan for long-lead submittals\n@refs: Egyptian General Conditions for Construction Works Contracts — Articles (19) Inspection and samples, (23) Drawings, (24) Examination of the site, (25) Possession of the site, (26) Site handover, (34) Contractor's representative, (35) Contractor's staff, (42) Setting out, (46) Programme, (47) Cash flow, (48) Commencement date | FIDIC Red Book 2017 — Sub-Clauses 2.1 Right of Access to the Site, 4.7 Setting Out, 8.1 Commencement of Works, 8.3 Programme\n\n## Contractual obligations at the start\n### Handing over and taking over the site\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (25) \"Possession of the site\"**: The Employer shall give the contractor possession of the whole site **immediately after signing the contract**, so that he can start at once in accordance with the programme. If he fails to do so and the contractor is shown to have incurred additional cost as a result, **the Employer bears that cost and grants an extension equal to the period of delay** in handing over the site.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (26) \"Site handover\"**: The Employer shall notify the contractor in writing of the date for taking over the site, and upon handover **a record is drawn up** stating the date of possession, the condition of the site and any other necessary data. If the contractor or his representative does not attend on that date, it is nevertheless deemed the commencement date, provided the requirements of Article (48) are met.\nKEY> The site handover record (see its form in \"Site Finishing & Execution\", Chapter 1) is **the first contractual document** in the file. Any obstruction recorded in it (occupation, utilities, encroachments) is a ready basis for an extension of time if its removal is delayed.\n### The commencement date\nUnder Article (48) time runs from **whichever is later**: handover of the site free of obstructions, or payment of the advance — provided the building permit has been obtained. Record both dates in a formal letter to the consultant confirming the **calculated commencement date** and the resulting contractual completion date, and ask for confirmation.\nEX> The contractor took over the site on 22/02/2026 and the advance was paid on 01/03/2026. Commencement = 01/03/2026 (the later). Contract period 14 months → completion = 30/04/2027. The technical office sends a letter with this calculation in the first week of March; if the consultant does not object, the calculation stands on the file.\n### Programme and cash flow\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (46) \"Programme of works\"**: On being notified to commence, the contractor shall submit within the period stated in the particular conditions a detailed programme for executing the works within the time for completion; the Engineer shall review and approve it, **and the programme is a condition of the contract** that the contractor must respect. If progress does not follow it, the contractor shall, on the Engineer's written instruction, submit a revised programme. The Engineer's approval of the programme does not relieve the contractor of his contractual obligations.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (47) \"Cash flow programme\"**: With the programme the contractor shall submit his preliminary cash-flow estimate showing the payments due under the programme, updated by periodic reports **at least quarterly**.\nCODE> **FIDIC 2017 — Sub-Clause 8.3**: The contractor submits an initial programme within **28 days** of receiving the notice under Sub-Clause 8.1, and a revised programme whenever the programme ceases to reflect actual progress or the contractor's obligations.\nThe programme has its own chapter in this book. At the start it is enough to know that the approved programme is **the baseline** against which any later delay is measured — so make sure it shows **the dates on which you need things from the consultant and the owner**: the approval date for each material, the date for each missing drawing, and the delivery date for each owner-supplied item.\n### Representative and staff\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (34) \"Contractor's representative on site\"**: The contractor shall appoint a **registered engineer specialised** in the field of the works as his authorised agent, present on site **permanently**, and notify the Engineer of his name **immediately after signing for approval**. The contractor may not consider himself his own technical representative on site even if he has the required experience.\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (35) \"Contractor's staff on site\"**: The contractor shall provide the necessary engineers, assistants and experienced technicians, competent supervisors and labour, and submit to the Engineer a detailed statement of his supervisory staff and workers and their specialisations in the form prepared by the Engineer.\n### Setting out and reference points\nCODE> **Egyptian General Conditions — Article (42) \"Setting out\"**: The contractor is responsible for setting out the works on site in the presence of the Engineer or his representative and for establishing levels according to **the reference points and dimensions given to him by the Engineer in writing**; he is responsible for their accuracy and for correcting any error at his own cost, unless the error results from wrong information given by the Engineer in writing, in which case the Employer bears the cost. The contractor is responsible for preserving all reference points (benchmarks and setting-out points) and restoring them if damaged.\nTIP> Request the reference points **by letter** and keep the reply. Article (42) makes the contractor bear setting-out errors except those caused by \"wrong information given by the Engineer in writing\" — and the key word is **in writing**.\n\n## Mobilisation documents for the first 30 days\nMobilisation is preparing the project to start: people, plant, site and documents. The table below lists the documents the technical office should normally submit in the first month (adjust the periods to the particular conditions):\nT: Proposed mobilisation document list for the first month\n| Document | Proposed deadline | Reference | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Performance security | Within 21 days of acceptance of tender | Art. 32 | Usually before signing |\n| Advance payment guarantee | With the advance payment request | Art. 76 | Fixes commencement if later than site handover |\n| Insurance policies | Per particular conditions | Art. 73 | With the Employer's approval of their terms (Art. 74) |\n| Appointment of the contractor's representative and CV | Immediately after signing | Art. 34 | — |\n| Organisation chart and staff statement | First week | Art. 35 | — |\n| Contract summary and time-limit table | First week | Internal | Chapter 2 |\n| Letter confirming commencement and completion dates | First week | Art. 48 | — |\n| Written request for reference points and benchmarks | First week | Art. 42 | — |\n| Temporary site layout | First two weeks | General expenses | Offices, stores, facilities |\n| Detailed programme + cash flow | Per particular conditions | Arts. 46 and 47 | — |\n| Project quality plan (PQP) | First month | Specification | Includes inspection and test plans |\n| Health, safety and environment plan | First month | Specification + law | — |\n| List of proposed subcontractors and suppliers | First month | Art. 9 | For written consent |\n| Register of drawings received + first batch of RFIs | First two weeks | Art. 23 | Result of the document review |\n| Submittal schedule | First month | Art. 19 | Ordered by the programme |\n| Agreed document numbering system | First week | — | Chapter 1 |\n\n## Studying the documents and finding conflicts\nBefore any work starts, the technical office goes through every drawing, the specification and the bill of quantities with one aim: **to find what will stop the work later** and get it settled now, \"in good time\" as Article (23) requires.\n### Document review checklist\n- [ ] Every drawing on the drawing list actually exists, at the stated revision.\n- [ ] Grids and levels agree between architectural and structural drawings.\n- [ ] Openings in walls and slabs (for pipes and ducts) appear in the structural drawings as in the MEP drawings.\n- [ ] Suspended-ceiling levels leave room for ducts, pipes and beams.\n- [ ] Door and window schedules agree with the plans and elevations.\n- [ ] A finishing schedule exists for every space and agrees with the BOQ.\n- [ ] Every BOQ item has a specification, and every specification has an item (or is included in one).\n- [ ] Items shown on the drawings but missing from the BOQ — recorded.\n- [ ] Materials specified by name: available on the market? Delivery time?\n- [ ] Tests, samples and mock-ups required — recorded in the submittal schedule.\n```fig 640x320\n[[\"r\",20,30,180,110,\"\",\"glass\"],[\"t\",110,50,\"Architectural\",13,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"r\",230,30,180,110,\"\",\"stone\"],[\"t\",320,50,\"Structural\",13,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"r\",440,30,180,110,\"\",\"zone\"],[\"t\",530,50,\"MEP\",13,\"#2b2620\",1],[\"c\",215,90,22,\"✕\",\"red\"],[\"c\",425,90,22,\"✕\",\"red\"],[\"ar\",215,112,300,200],[\"ar\",425,112,340,200],[\"r\",230,200,180,50,\"Request for information (RFI)\",\"white\"],[\"ar\",320,250,320,285],[\"r\",230,285,180,30,\"Written answer settles the conflict\",\"green\"],[\"t\",110,170,\"Ceiling level 2.70\",10,\"#6b6152\"],[\"t\",320,170,\"Beam soffit 2.75\",10,\"#6b6152\"],[\"t\",530,170,\"AC duct 30 cm\",10,\"#6b6152\"]]\n```\n: An example of a conflict between disciplines found by studying the documents and settled by an RFI before construction\n\n## The request for information (RFI)\n### Definition and purpose\nA **request for information** is a written question from the contractor to the Engineer to clarify an **ambiguity, conflict or gap** in the contract documents. Its purpose is not to \"complain\" but to **obtain a written decision** in time for construction.\n### When it is used\n- A conflict between two documents or two disciplines.\n- A missing detail without which work cannot proceed.\n- A specified material that is not available on the market.\n- A site condition that differs from the documents (level, obstruction, utility).\n### Rules for a good RFI\n1. **One question** per request (easier to answer and follow up).\n2. **Precise reference**: drawing number and revision, specification clause or BOQ item, location on site.\n3. **A photograph or extract of the drawing** showing the problem.\n4. **A proposed solution** from the contractor (it speeds up the answer and shows professionalism).\n5. **The date the answer is required**, linked to a programme activity.\n6. **The possible impact** on cost or time, if any — in one clear sentence that reserves the right without exaggeration.\n!> An RFI is not a variation order. The answer to an RFI may change cost or time, but it **is not a financial approval**. If the answer changes the scope, write to the consultant within days stating that the answer constitutes a variation under Article (56) and ask for a variation order.\n!> Do not use RFIs to shift design responsibility that belongs to the contractor (in design-and-build contracts), and do not flood the consultant with questions whose answers are clear in the documents; the document loses its value and the important questions are answered late.\n\n:::form Request for Information (RFI)\n| RFI no. | ____ | Date | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Project | ____ | Discipline | ____ |\n| To | ____ | From | ____ |\n| Subject | ____ | | |\nT:\n| Reference (drawing / revision / spec clause / BOQ item) | Location on site |\n|---|---|\n| ____ | ____ |\nDescription of the query: ____\nContractor's proposal: ____\nResponse required by: ____   Related programme activity: ____\nPossible impact:  [ ] None   [ ] Cost   [ ] Time   [ ] To be determined\nAttachments: ____\n| Submitted by (technical office) | ____ | | |\n|---|---|---|---|\nEngineer's answer: ____\n| Engineer / representative | ____ | Date of answer | ____ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\n\n:::filled Request for Information (RFI)\n| RFI no. | NCR26-CON-ME-RFI-0007 | Date | 09/03/2026 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Project | NCR-26 Residential and office building | Discipline | HVAC / architectural / structural |\n| To | Eng. Hossam Nabil — Resident Engineer | From | Technical Office — Modern Building |\n| Subject | Conflict between the suspended-ceiling level, the AC duct and beam B12 — typical floor corridor | | |\n| Reference (drawing / revision / spec clause / BOQ item) | Location on site |\n|---|---|\n| A-201 Rev.B (ceiling +2.70) — S-105 Rev.A (soffit of beam B12 +2.75) — M-301 Rev.A (duct 600×300) | Corridor, floors 1–5, between grids C/D |\nDescription of the query: The clear space between the soffit of beam B12 (+2.75) and the suspended-ceiling level (+2.70) is only 5 cm, while a 30 cm supply duct plus 5 cm for insulation and hangers runs under the beam. The ceiling cannot be installed at the stated level.\nContractor's proposal: (a) lower the suspended ceiling in the corridor only to +2.35 (the corridor's clear height remains above 2.20), or (b) reroute the duct between the beams in the other direction — needs review by the HVAC designer.\nResponse required by: 23/03/2026   Related programme activity: A2310 Suspended-ceiling framing — first floor (starts 05/04/2026)\nPossible impact:  [ ] None   [ ] Cost   [ ] Time   [x] To be determined\nAttachments: Proposed section SK-07 + site photograph of beam B12.\n| Submitted by (technical office) | Eng. Omar Khamis | | |\n|---|---|---|---|\nEngineer's answer: Solution (a) is approved: the suspended ceiling in the corridors only is lowered to +2.35, other ceilings remain unchanged. The contractor shall submit a revised suspended-ceiling shop drawing for floors 1–5.\n| Engineer / representative | Eng. Hossam Nabil | Date of answer | 18/03/2026 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n:::\nEX> Note that the answer came on 18/03/2026 — before the required date. Had it come after 05/04/2026 and stopped the ceiling framing, the RFI log (required date + related activity + actual answer date) would have been complete evidence for an extension of time under Article (23), paragraph 5.\n\n## The kick-off meeting\nThe first formal meeting of owner, consultant and contractor after signing. Its purpose is to **agree the rules of work** before the work itself begins.\n### Proposed agenda\n1. Introduction of the parties, their representatives and their authority (the powers of the Engineer and his representative under Articles 14 and 15).\n2. Commencement and completion dates.\n3. Communication: who sends to whom, notice addresses, approved e-mail, numbering system.\n4. Dates for submitting the programme and the quality and safety plans.\n5. Procedures and review periods for submittals and inspection requests.\n6. Regular meetings: weekly on site, monthly at management level.\n7. Format and timing of reports (daily, weekly, monthly).\n8. Payment procedure: monthly date, format and attachments.\n9. Site handover, reference points, temporary facilities, working hours.\n10. Items supplied by the owner or by nominated subcontractors.\n11. Any conflicts or gaps known so far.\n\n:::filled Kick-off Meeting Minutes\n| Project | NCR-26 Residential and office building | Minutes no. | NCR26-ENG-GN-MOM-0001 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Date and place | 03/03/2026 — site office — 11:00 am | Prepared by | Integrated Engineering Office (consultant) |\n| Attendees | Owner: Eng. Tarek Fouad — Consultant: Eng. Hossam Nabil, Eng. Rania Adel — Contractor: Eng. Khaled Saeed, Eng. Omar Khamis | | |\n| No. | Subject | Agreed | Action by / date |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Commencement and completion | Commencement 01/03/2026 (advance paid) — completion 30/04/2027 | Consultant confirms in writing — 05/03/2026 |\n| 2 | Authority | Eng. Hossam Nabil is the Engineer's representative with authority for technical inspection and approval; financial variation orders signed by the consultant's director with the owner's approval | Consultant sends letter of delegation — 05/03/2026 |\n| 3 | Communication | All correspondence by numbered letter + PDF copy to the approved e-mail; contractor's proposed numbering system accepted | Contractor — immediately |\n| 4 | Programme | To be submitted within 21 days of notice to commence (by 22/03/2026) in Primavera P6 + PDF | Contractor — 22/03/2026 |\n| 5 | Submittals | Review period 14 days; long-lead items (lifts, mixers, aluminium) submitted first | Contractor — submittal schedule 15/03/2026 |\n| 6 | Inspection requests | Sent at least 24 hours ahead; inspections daily 9 am – 3 pm | Contractor — ongoing |\n| 7 | Meetings | Weekly every Sunday 12 noon on site; monthly on the first Thursday at the owner's office | Consultant prepares minutes |\n| 8 | Payments | Submitted on the 25th of each month with measurement books and photographs | Contractor — monthly |\n| 9 | Conflicts | Corridor conflict (ceiling/duct/beam) — RFI-0007 sent | Consultant — answer before 23/03/2026 |\nThese minutes are deemed correct unless objected to in writing within 7 days of receipt.\n| Owner | Eng. Tarek Fouad | Consultant | Eng. Hossam Nabil |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Contractor | Eng. Khaled Saeed | | |\n:::\nTIP> When the consultant prepares the minutes, review them as soon as they arrive and send your comments **in writing within the period** stated at the end. Minutes not objected to are deemed correct, even if they leave out what you said in the meeting.\n\n## The long-lead submittal plan\nSome materials take weeks or months from approval to arrival: lifts, fabricated aluminium and glass, imported marble, mixers and sanitary ware, switchboards and air-conditioning units. These must be **the first items submitted for approval**.\nT: Example submittal plan for long-lead items (project NCR-26)\n| Item | Submitted | Expected approval (14 days) | Manufacture and delivery | Required on site | Float |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lifts (2 no.) | 15/03/2026 | 29/03/2026 | 20 weeks | 01/09/2026 | 2 weeks |\n| Aluminium sections and glazing | 22/03/2026 | 05/04/2026 | 10 weeks | 01/07/2026 | 2 weeks |\n| Mixers and sanitary ware (imported) | 01/04/2026 | 15/04/2026 | 12 weeks | 01/10/2026 | 12 weeks |\n| Central AC units | 29/03/2026 | 12/04/2026 | 14 weeks | 01/08/2026 | 2 weeks |\n| Porcelain floor tiles | 12/04/2026 | 26/04/2026 | 6 weeks | 15/07/2026 | 5 weeks |\nKEY> Little float (two weeks) means any late approval will delay the project. Put these items at the top of the submittal log, follow their answers daily, and record any late answer as soon as it happens.\n\n## Checklist for the first six weeks\n- [ ] Site handover record signed, with obstructions recorded.\n- [ ] Commencement and completion dates confirmed in writing.\n- [ ] Guarantees and insurance policies submitted.\n- [ ] Contractor's representative and staff approved.\n- [ ] Reference points and benchmarks handed over in writing.\n- [ ] Contract summary and time-limit table prepared and distributed.\n- [ ] Drawing register complete and every drawing stamped with its revision.\n- [ ] Document review finished and the first RFIs sent.\n- [ ] Kick-off meeting held and its minutes agreed.\n- [ ] Programme and cash flow submitted on time.\n- [ ] Quality plan and safety plan submitted.\n- [ ] Submittal schedule submitted, with long-lead items first.\n- [ ] All registers (correspondence, drawings, submittals, RFI, IR) open and up to date.\n\nGL: التعبئة | Mobilization | Preparing the project with people, plant, facilities and documents to start\nGL: الاجتماع الافتتاحي | Kick-off meeting | The first formal meeting of the project parties to agree the rules of work\nGL: خطة الاعتمادات | Submittal schedule | A schedule of submission and approval dates for every material and subcontractor, linked to the programme\nGL: البنود طويلة التوريد | Long-lead items | Materials with a long period between approval and arrival on site\nGL: الثوابت | Reference points | Setting-out and level reference points handed by the Engineer to the contractor\n"}]};
